WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.076 --> 00:00:14.979
"One thousand repeated Lawyer Tolman solemnly, "and here is the money."

2
00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:21.581
Young Gillian gave an amused laugh as he fingered the thin package of new fifty-dollar notes.

3
00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:28.203
"You heard the reading of your uncle's will," continued Lawyer Tolman, professionally impersonal in his tone.

4
00:00:28.223 --> 00:00:32.424
"I do not know if you paid much attention to its details.

5
00:00:33.156 --> 00:00:34.517
I must remind you of one.

6
00:00:35.377 --> 00:00:41.419
You are required to render to us an account of the expenditure of this thousand dollars as soon

7
00:00:41.459 --> 00:00:42.740
as you have disposed of it.

8
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.361
I trust that you will comply with the late Mr.

9
00:00:46.401 --> 00:00:47.262
Gillian's wishes."

10
00:00:48.242 --> 00:00:51.243
"You may depend upon it," said the young man politely.

11
00:00:52.384 --> 00:00:53.644
Gillian went to his club.

12
00:00:54.285 --> 00:00:57.226
There he hunted out one whom he called Old Bryson.

13
00:00:58.286 --> 00:01:02.128
Old Bryson was calm and forty and preferred to be left alone.

14
00:01:03.124 --> 00:01:09.367
He was in a corner reading a book, and when he saw Gillian approaching, he sighed, laid down

15
00:01:09.427 --> 00:01:11.648
his book, and took off his glasses.

16
00:01:13.308 --> 00:01:15.869
"Old Bryson, wake up," said Gillian.

17
00:01:15.889 --> 00:01:18.110
"I've a funny story to tell you."

18
00:01:19.031 --> 00:01:22.572
"I wish you would tell it to someone else," said Old Bryson.

19
00:01:23.052 --> 00:01:25.033
"You know how I hate your stories."

20
00:01:25.993 --> 00:01:28.895
"This is a better one than usual," said Gillian.

21
00:01:28.915 --> 00:01:32.216
"I've just come from my late uncle's legal firm.

22
00:01:33.044 --> 00:01:34.825
He leaves me a thousand dollars.

23
00:01:35.545 --> 00:01:39.406
Now, what can a man possibly do with a thousand dollars?"

24
00:01:39.426 --> 00:01:46.908
"I thought," said Old Bryson, showing as much interest as a bee shows in vinegar, "that the late

25
00:01:47.128 --> 00:01:50.369
Septimus Gillian was worth something like half a million."

26
00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:55.110
"He was," agreed Gillian, "and that's where the joke comes in.

27
00:01:55.990 --> 00:02:01.032
He's left part of it to the man who discovers a new type of bacteria and the rest

28
00:02:01.304 --> 00:02:04.346
to establish a hospital for doing away with it again.

29
00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:09.330
The butler and the housekeeper get a seal ring and ten dollars each.

30
00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:11.691
His nephew gets a thousand dollars."

31
00:02:11.711 --> 00:02:17.295
"You've always had plenty of money to spend," observed Old Bryson.

32
00:02:18.096 --> 00:02:19.737
"Tons," said Gillian.

33
00:02:19.757 --> 00:02:23.860
"Uncle was the fairy godmother as far as an allowance was concerned."

34
00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.781
"Any other heirs?"

35
00:02:26.341 --> 00:02:27.182
asked Old Bryson.

36
00:02:27.202 --> 00:02:29.964
"None." Gillian frowned uneasily.

37
00:02:30.732 --> 00:02:35.274
"There is a Miss Hayden, a ward of my uncle, who lived in his house.

38
00:02:35.874 --> 00:02:36.895
She's a quiet thing.

39
00:02:37.755 --> 00:02:42.257
I forgot to say that she was in on the seal-ring-and-ten-dollars joke too.

40
00:02:43.317 --> 00:02:48.919
Don't be superior and insulting, Old Bryson—tell me what a fellow can do with a thousand dollars."

41
00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:52.101
Old Bryson rubbed his glasses and smiled.

42
00:02:52.921 --> 00:02:58.143
And when Old Bryson smiled, Gillian knew that he intended to be more offensive than ever.

43
00:02:58.163 --> 00:03:02.706
"A thousand dollars," he said, "means much or little.

44
00:03:03.607 --> 00:03:07.210
One man may buy a happy home with it and laugh at Rockefeller.

45
00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:12.134
Another could send his wife for a vacation with it and save her life.

46
00:03:13.415 --> 00:03:20.520
A thousand dollars would buy pure milk for one hundred babies during June, July, and August and save

47
00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.741
fifty of their lives.

48
00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:25.645
It would provide an education to an ambitious boy."

49
00:03:25.665 --> 00:03:32.751
"People might like you, Old Bryson," said Gillian, always unruffled, "if you wouldn't moralize.

50
00:03:33.671 --> 00:03:36.894
I asked you to tell me what I could do with a thousand dollars."

51
00:03:36.914 --> 00:03:40.317
"You?" said Bryson with a gentle laugh.

52
00:03:40.337 --> 00:03:45.522
"Why, Bobby Gillian, there's only one logical thing you could do.

53
00:03:46.322 --> 00:03:49.745
You can go buy Miss Lotta Lauriere a diamond pendant."

54
00:03:49.765 --> 00:03:52.468
"Thanks," said Gillian, rising.

55
00:03:52.488 --> 00:03:55.490
"I thought I could depend upon you, Old Bryson.

56
00:03:56.111 --> 00:03:57.412
You've got the right idea.

57
00:03:58.168 --> 00:04:02.492
I wanted to spend the money in a lump sum, for I've got to turn in an account

58
00:04:02.552 --> 00:04:04.433
for it, and I hate itemizing."

59
00:04:05.094 --> 00:04:09.177
Gillian phoned for a cab and said to the driver: "The Columbine Theatre."

60
00:04:10.018 --> 00:04:16.023
Miss Lotta Lauriere was almost ready for her call at a crowded matinee, when her dresser mentioned the

61
00:04:16.063 --> 00:04:16.683
name of Mr.

62
00:04:16.703 --> 00:04:19.525
Gillian. "Now, what is it, Bobby?"

63
00:04:20.066 --> 00:04:21.087
said Miss Lauriere.

64
00:04:21.447 --> 00:04:22.868
"I'm going on in two minutes."

65
00:04:22.888 --> 00:04:24.970
"It won't take two minutes.

66
00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:27.492
What do you say to a little pendant?

67
00:04:27.964 --> 00:04:29.044
I can spend a grand."

68
00:04:30.045 --> 00:04:32.725
"Oh, just as you say," replied Miss Lauriere.

69
00:04:32.745 --> 00:04:37.667
"Say, Bobby, did you see that necklace Della Stacey had on the other night?

70
00:04:38.347 --> 00:04:40.808
Twenty-two hundred dollars it cost at Tiffany's.

71
00:04:40.908 --> 00:04:44.129
But—" "Miss Lauriere for the opening chorus!"

72
00:04:44.549 --> 00:04:45.489
cried the callboy.

73
00:04:46.129 --> 00:04:48.690
Gillian strolled out to where his cab was waiting.

74
00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.371
"What would you do with a thousand dollars?"

75
00:04:51.891 --> 00:04:52.811
he asked the driver.

76
00:04:52.831 --> 00:04:56.092
"Open a saloon," said the cabby promptly.

77
00:04:56.828 --> 00:04:59.590
"I know a place I could take money in with both hands.

78
00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:02.192
It's a four-story brick on a corner.

79
00:05:02.992 --> 00:05:11.098
I've got it figured out: second story, Chinese food; third floor, manicures; fourth floor, poolroom.

80
00:05:11.698 --> 00:05:17.963
If you're thinking of putting up the capital—" "Oh no," said Gillian, "I merely asked from curiosity.

81
00:05:18.523 --> 00:05:19.624
I'll pay you by the hour.

82
00:05:20.044 --> 00:05:21.585
Drive 'til I tell you to stop."

83
00:05:22.906 --> 00:05:25.528
Eight blocks down Broadway, Gillian got out.

84
00:05:26.192 --> 00:05:29.695
A blind man sat upon a stool on the sidewalk selling pencils.

85
00:05:30.316 --> 00:05:32.157
Gillian went out and stood before him.

86
00:05:33.258 --> 00:05:38.102
"Excuse me," he said, "but would you mind telling me what you would do if you had a

87
00:05:38.162 --> 00:05:38.882
thousand dollars?"

88
00:05:38.902 --> 00:05:42.706
"Take a look at that, if you like," said the blind man.

89
00:05:43.566 --> 00:05:46.969
He drew a bank deposit book from his coat pocket and held it out.

90
00:05:47.489 --> 00:05:48.911
It showed a total of $1,785.

91
00:05:48.951 --> 00:05:50.192
Gillian got back into the cab.

92
00:05:50.212 --> 00:05:56.112
"I forgot something," he said.

93
00:05:56.132 --> 00:06:00.535
"You may drive to the law offices of Tolman Sharp, at Broadway."

94
00:06:01.355 --> 00:06:07.018
Back at the office, Tolman looked at him hostilely and inquiringly through his gold-rimmed glasses.

95
00:06:07.038 --> 00:06:12.661
"I beg your pardon," said Gillian cheerfully, "but may I ask you a question?

96
00:06:13.281 --> 00:06:15.303
It is not an impertinent one, I hope.

97
00:06:15.843 --> 00:06:20.525
Was Miss Hayden left anything by my uncle's will besides the ring and the ten dollars?"

98
00:06:21.346 --> 00:06:22.506
"Nothing," said Mr.

99
00:06:22.546 --> 00:06:28.426
Tolman. "I thank you very much, sir," said Gillian, and on he went to his cab.

100
00:06:28.946 --> 00:06:31.768
He gave the driver the address of his late uncle's home.

101
00:06:32.689 --> 00:06:35.031
Miss Hayden was writing letters in the library.

102
00:06:35.631 --> 00:06:38.413
She was small and slender and clothed in black.

103
00:06:39.554 --> 00:06:41.075
But you would have noticed her eyes.

104
00:06:41.956 --> 00:06:45.738
Gillian drifted in with his air of regarding the world as inconsequential.

105
00:06:45.758 --> 00:06:48.981
"I've just come from old Tolman's," he explained.

106
00:06:49.561 --> 00:06:51.583
"They've been going over the papers down there.

107
00:06:52.603 --> 00:06:58.930
They found a"—Gillian searched his memory for a legal term—"they found a postscript or something to the will.

108
00:06:59.911 --> 00:07:02.712
It seems that the old boy willed you a thousand dollars.

109
00:07:03.392 --> 00:07:05.093
Tolman asked me to bring you the money.

110
00:07:05.933 --> 00:07:06.353
Here it is.

111
00:07:06.813 --> 00:07:08.694
You'd better count it to see if it's right."

112
00:07:09.334 --> 00:07:10.895
Gillian laid the money on the desk.

113
00:07:11.976 --> 00:07:13.076
Miss Hayden turned white.

114
00:07:13.856 --> 00:07:16.617
"Oh!" she said, and again, "Oh!"

115
00:07:17.558 --> 00:07:19.739
Gillian half turned and looked out the window.

116
00:07:21.199 --> 00:07:25.850
"I suppose, of course," he said, in a low voice, "that you know I love you."

117
00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:29.773
"I'm sorry," said Miss Hayden, taking up her money.

118
00:07:29.793 --> 00:07:32.995
"So there is no hope for us?"

119
00:07:33.755 --> 00:07:35.316
asked Gillian, almost lightheartedly.

120
00:07:35.336 --> 00:07:38.258
"I am sorry," she said again.

121
00:07:38.278 --> 00:07:40.180
"May I write a note?"

122
00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:42.101
asked Gillian, with a smile.

123
00:07:42.701 --> 00:07:46.544
She supplied him with paper and pen, and then went back to her desk.

124
00:07:47.345 --> 00:07:53.784
Gillian made out his account of his expenditure of the thousand dollars in these words: "Paid by the

125
00:07:53.824 --> 00:08:00.887
black sheep, Robert Gillian, one thousand dollars on account of the eternal happiness owed by heaven to the

126
00:08:00.927 --> 00:08:02.828
best and dearest woman on earth."

127
00:08:03.828 --> 00:08:07.750
Gillian slipped his writing into an envelope, bowed, and went his way.

128
00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:11.832
His cab stopped again at the offices of Tolman Sharp.

129
00:08:11.852 --> 00:08:16.894
"I have spent the thousand dollars," he said cheerily to Tolman.

130
00:08:17.634 --> 00:08:20.075
He tossed a white envelope on the lawyer's table.

131
00:08:20.095 --> 00:08:24.477
"You will find there an account, sir, of the vanishing of the dollars."

132
00:08:25.437 --> 00:08:27.418
Without touching the envelope, Mr.

133
00:08:27.478 --> 00:08:30.139
Tolman went to a door and called his partner, Sharp.

134
00:08:30.899 --> 00:08:33.661
Together they explored the caverns of an immense safe.

135
00:08:34.581 --> 00:08:40.904
From the safe, they dragged a big envelope sealed with wax, which they invaded, and wagged their venerable

136
00:08:40.964 --> 00:08:42.844
heads together over its contents.

137
00:08:43.865 --> 00:08:45.546
Then Tolman became spokesman.

138
00:08:46.486 --> 00:08:50.908
"Mr. Gillian," he said formally, "there was a supplement to your uncle's will.

139
00:08:51.644 --> 00:08:57.406
It was entrusted to us privately, with instructions that it not be opened until you had furnished us

140
00:08:57.706 --> 00:09:00.426
with a full account of your handling of the thousand dollars.

141
00:09:01.527 --> 00:09:04.167
I will acquaint you with the spirit of its contents.

142
00:09:05.308 --> 00:09:11.309
"In the event that your expenditure of the thousand dollars demonstrates that you possess any of the qualifications

143
00:09:11.569 --> 00:09:15.030
that deserve reward, much benefit will come to you.

144
00:09:15.951 --> 00:09:18.131
Mr. Sharp and I are named as the judges.

145
00:09:18.851 --> 00:09:25.350
If your disposal of the money in question has been prudent, wise, or unselfish, it is in our

146
00:09:25.451 --> 00:09:28.052
power to hand you fifty thousand dollars.

147
00:09:29.173 --> 00:09:34.896
But if you have used this money foolishly, the fifty thousand is to be paid to Miriam Hayden

148
00:09:35.196 --> 00:09:36.037
without delay.

149
00:09:37.077 --> 00:09:37.678
Now, Mr.

150
00:09:37.718 --> 00:09:38.978
Gillian, Mr.

151
00:09:39.038 --> 00:09:42.821
Sharp and I will examine your account in regard to the thousand dollars."

152
00:09:43.741 --> 00:09:45.642
Mr. Tolman reached for the envelope.

153
00:09:46.363 --> 00:09:48.484
Gillian was a little quicker in taking it up.

154
00:09:49.204 --> 00:09:53.807
He slowly tore the account and its cover into strips and dropped them into his pocket.

155
00:09:53.827 --> 00:09:57.249
"It's all right," he said, smilingly.

156
00:09:57.269 --> 00:10:00.632
"There isn't a bit of need to bother you with this.

157
00:10:01.472 --> 00:10:03.794
I lost the thousand dollars on the horse races.

158
00:10:04.214 --> 00:10:05.275
Good day, gentlemen."

159
00:10:06.295 --> 00:10:12.159
Tolman and Sharp shook their heads mournfully at each other when Gillian left, for they heard him whistling

160
00:10:12.219 --> 00:10:14.981
gaily in the hallway as he waited for the elevator.
